{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788491674,"nonce":"ljzsbGxu2fGv+JyvZZW4Zp1PFMs=","title":"The cockpit (wave 6): the agent does not choose what to look at","message":"Root of the cockpit round. The design is RUNS/openrun-20260902/audit/COCKPIT.md;\nthe pass over every open issue that produced this round is\nRUNS/openrun-20260902/audit/ISSUES-DISPOSITION.md. Read COCKPIT.md first. This\nissue carries only what the design does not: the parts, the order, and the\nmeasurement.\n\nWHAT\n\nEvery turn starts with a screen the mod built, delivered as the reply to\n`advance`. The clock stops for what the game stops for, plus a destroyed\nbuilding and a deadline. Chores are done by the mod without a turn. Decisions\narrive framed with their acts ready to send, and hold the clock until answered\nor deferred with a reason.\n\nWhy this round exists, in one line each, from the audit of openrun-20260902:\na truthful field was read zero times in 52 chances (T0); destruction is not a\njournal event and the lost autocannon is the first link in the wipe; the rule to\nlook at the base went from 22 uses to 2 across the run (T11); the read gate was\ndefeated four ways (T4); 22 human interventions are in no record (T14).\n\nTHE PARTS — each its own spec, ids in comment #1\n\n- the screen: advance replies with what a player would be looking at\n- the map: a picture every turn, chosen by the mod, drawn the way the game draws it\n- the chores: what the mod does without a turn\n- who did it, and what was destroyed: provenance and the destroy hook\n- honest instruments: ok means what you asked for happened\n- the client: prints the screen, renders the picture, never loses a result\n\nORDER\n\nWho-did-it first (the screen depends on it), then the screen, the map, the\nchores. Honest instruments and the client as their items come up. Two things\nland now regardless: e440676 (error classes, built, never benched) and\nseekandkill a6b1aa0 (a null guard at a named line).\n\nACCEPTANCE — the round, not the parts\n\n- A run of the M1 contract (664e9b9) is played on the cockpit: every advance\n  replied with a screen, every screen written to RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/frames/.\n- Protocol steps per in-game day are measured and compared with this run's ~95\n  (7f0e245's number). The number is reported whatever it is.\n- Zero decisions lapsed: every letter with a choice, every joiner or refugee\n  offer, every alert older than a day is answered or carries a journaled\n  deferral with a reason.\n- Every human intervention during the run appears in the human row of the next\n  screen.\n- Something destroyed during the run appears on the map as gone and on a gauge\n  as \"was N\" until it is rebuilt or written off.\n- The harness log is read for whether the agent quoted screen lines and opened\n  the picture. Both are UNKNOWN today (COCKPIT.md §What is UNKNOWN) and the\n  numbers go in the run's post-mortem, whatever they are.\n\nABSORBS\n\n7f0e245 — its written audit (\"every multi-tick process and every standing rule,\nmarked until-matcher / mod-side / stays with the agent\") is COCKPIT.md's line\ntable; its list A is answered by the stops and by `until:{condition}` predicates\nover screen fields; its measurement is the second acceptance bullet above; its\ncomment #2 (tend until stable, the Doctor row excluding the patient) goes to the\nchores and to aa4391b.","files":null}]}